{"reference":"Daniel 5","book":"Daniel","chapter":5,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Historical and Textual Questions","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"This chapter’s final banquet is associated with Belshazzar, and inscriptions independently confirm that a historical Belshazzar existed as Nabonidus’s son and held high authority. They do not, however, settle every question raised by the chapter’s presentation of him as king, its family language, or its chronology. The following notice that Darius the Mede received the kingdom also remains unresolved: no independently attested ruler securely matches all of Daniel’s descriptions. Daniel also survives in substantially different Old Greek and Theodotionic forms, especially in chapters 4–6; their differences need to be considered passage by passage rather than assigned one simple cause.","verse_refs":["Daniel 5:1-31","Daniel 5:30-31"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_daniel_005_4d32715adbb4ed1299ec57c2","reader_path_daniel_005_f5c29e542cd58d22f245b801"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}